# Stability

## What it means

A sort is **stable** when it preserves the relative order of elements that
compare equal. Sort by one key, then by another, and a stable sort leaves the
first key's order intact within each group of the second. ^definition

Given records sorted by name, sorting by department with a stable sort leaves
each department's names in alphabetical order. An unstable sort scrambles them,
and nothing in its output says that it did.

```d2
direction: right

before: "Sorted by name" {
  a: "Ada — Eng"
  b: "Bo — Eng"
  c: "Cy — Ops"
}

stable: "Stable sort by dept" {
  s1: "Ada — Eng"
  s2: "Bo — Eng"
  s3: "Cy — Ops"
  style.stroke: "#2e7d32"
}

unstable: "Unstable sort by dept" {
  u1: "Bo — Eng"
  u2: "Ada — Eng"
  u3: "Cy — Ops"
  style.stroke: "#c62828"
}

before -> stable: "names still ordered"
before -> unstable: "names scrambled"
```

## Which of the five are stable

Stable: [[bubble-sort]], [[insertion-sort]], [[merge-sort]]. All three move an
element past another only when they compare unequal.

Unstable: [[quicksort]] and [[heapsort]]. Both swap distant elements, and a swap
across a run of equal keys reorders them with nothing to record that it
happened.

!!! note "Instability is a choice, not an accident"

    Quicksort's partition and heapsort's sift both gain their speed by moving
    elements a long way in one step. That is the same property that loses
    stability. You can make either stable by carrying an index as a tiebreaker,
    at the cost of $O(n)$ extra memory — which is merge sort's cost, for merge
    sort's guarantee, so at that point use [[merge-sort]].

## When it matters

- **Multi-key sorts.** Sort by the least significant key first, then the most
  significant. This only works if every pass after the first is stable.
- **Idempotent output.** Sorting an already-sorted list must not change it —
  useful when the output is committed, diffed, or hashed.
- **Attached data.** When equal keys carry different payloads, "equal" elements
  are not interchangeable to the reader even though they are to the comparator.

It does not matter when keys are unique, which is why it is easy to forget until
a report changes order between two runs with no code change in between.
